  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Solve the inequality $-1<\\frac{6x-8}{2}<1.$",
  "worked_solution": "We isolate $x$ by applying the addition and multiplication principles, as usual.\n\nFirst, we multiply by $2$ to eliminate the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & <\\frac{6x-8}{2}<1 \\\\ -1\\cdot 2 & <\\frac{6x-8}{2}\\cdot 2<1\\cdot 2 \\\\ -2 & <6x-8<2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we continue isolating $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & <6x-8<2 \\\\ -2+8 & <6x-8+8<2+8 \\\\ 6 & <6x<10 \\\\ \\frac{6}{6} & <\\frac{6x}{6}<\\frac{10}{6} \\\\ 1 & <x<\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $1<x<\\frac{5}{3}.$",
